			<div class="content" id="post-content-2520348">
									

<p>A short piece related to some Chinese myth. Great thanks to Athena_Grey, Metaphysician, Lex1nat0r, Jekeled, TwistedGears, Soulless, minmin, MrWrong, Ireul, ARD, Faminepulse and all the others who reviewed the skip.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Related&nbsp;Myths</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Related&nbsp;Myths</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>Yu the Great/大禹, the first king of Xia Dynasty, once dealt with great floods.</li>
<li>Sun Wukong/孙悟空's weapon, Ruyi Jingu Bang/如意金箍棒, was originally used by Yu during his flood control, and was called 定海神针.</li>
<li>Yu the Great fought a water monster during his flood control, who he defeated and locked under a well. (In some versions of the myth, the well is in Mount Tongbai) One version goes that the monster is ape-like, called 巫支祁, who said to be one of the prototypes Sun Wukong based on. The other version goes that the monster is a Jiaolong (an aquatic dragon, but less powerful and divine). [禹王锁蛟]</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>There are (at least) two different interpretations of what happened, based on which version of the myth it uses. Other interpretations are aslo possible, as the piece is intended to be open-ended.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">First version:</span><br />
Sun Wukong was defeated by Yu and locked in a well, he escaped after the chains become weak. He stole Yu's needle because he hated him, and crossed out the original text, engraving his own. Years later, he converted to Buddism (after maybe going through what happened in Journey to the West), and realized his wrong doings. He returned to where he was imprisoned, and passed away there. He could have simply died, or he could have abandoned his mortal body and ascended to godhood (became a Buddha).</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Second version:</span><br />
Sun Wukong stumbled upon Yu's needle by chance and claimed it his own. As he didn't know better and was arrogant, he crossed out the original text, engraving his own. Years later, he converted to Buddism (after maybe going through what happened in Journey to the West), and began to do good. At that time, the dragon/snake monster, who was imprisoned by Yu was about to break out, feeling that he owed Yu a great debt, he fought the dragon and in the end, used the needle to covert the water of the lake, imprision the dragon again. The mudslide was cause not by the lake's conversion back into water, but by the dragon escaping.</p>
<p>Minor Points that did not make into the piece, but are what I had in mind:</p>
<ul>
<li>Fuxi is what Xia Dynasty snake people call the Broken God. The needle is a Broken God relic, remodified by Yu the Great to control floods. (Putting it into the rivers will obviously immediately stop the flood :P) It might originally have other anomalous properties.</li>
<li>The Xia Dynasty snake people don't see the Broken God/Mekhane/Fuxi as broken &#8212; they think it used itself to trap Yaldabaoth. Thus they see Broken God relics as the tools Broken God used or things manifested by its mind.</li>
<li>The well was spared from the massive reality wipe the Xia Dynasty went through, is because the chains originally had reality stablizing ability &#8212; it is composed of the same material with <a href="/scp-2481">SCP-2481</a>-2. But they later lost that property because of the long duration of time.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>

