				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607805836" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1465321758 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Jun 2016 17:49</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2520654">
									

<p>I actually sat down and figured out the whole process because I am a total nerd for engineering.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">You'd&nbsp;want&nbsp;to&nbsp;do&nbsp;something&nbsp;like&nbsp;this.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Warning:&nbsp;critical&nbsp;nerdery&nbsp;levels&nbsp;detected.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Basically, this combines some tricks they use for drilling into ice in Antarctica, where water can re-freeze in seconds, others they use for drilling oil wells and tunnels into hard but unstable rock, and some standard pier construction methods.</p>
<p>This assumes they've figured out that water reverts when separated from the anomaly, and that it's probably related to the thing they can see a couple meters under the surface. From there, what you do is:</p>
<ol>
<li>Helicopter in a small heavy-duty drilling rig. (These do actually exist. They have mining, quarrying, and construction applications.)</li>
<li>Mount rig on a flat barge and drag to just above the anomaly under the lake.</li>
<li>Drill set of small bore holes through lake around anomaly; hammer in anchor piles.
<ul>
<li>this assumes &quot;what happens to the rest of the lake when the anomaly is gone&quot; has occurred to them</li>
<li>As with arctic drilling, the neat part is that the &quot;melting&quot; water acts as both slurry and coolant. Also like arctic drilling, a fast-cycle pump with a set of chambered valves quickly sucks out liquid water before it can &quot;refreeze&quot; and wreak havoc on your machinery.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Drill a pilot hole to just above Sun Wukong, and line the whole thing with pipe as you go to keep your hole open.</li>
<li>switch to large tunnel-boring bit. Using the first pipe as a guide, expand the hole and insert utility pipe sections. At the end, you have a roughly 2.25 meter hole lined with pipe above the anomaly, preferably big enough for a reseus macaque and a large chunk of iron-water to fit through. Cap this with a watertight seal. Last thing you need is a freak rainstorm refilling a week's worth of drilling.</li>
<li>Drill a second hole like this, but at an angle landing it a safe distance underneath SW.</li>
<li>Now comes the tricky part. Carefully drill a series of holes around SW, connecting the upper and lower tunnels.</li>
<li>Ream out the area between these holes, and gradually build a complete watertight connection between the upper and lower tunnels, leaving only a thin link of Iron-water between the rest of the lake and the now isolated chunk around SW.</li>
<li>Run a final check that your anchor columns are up to the job of supporting your construction project in normal water. You're starting over if you get this wrong.</li>
<li>sever the connection between the chunk and the lake.</li>
<li>Slam a plug into the hole to keep the water from flooding in faster than you can pull the now-free chunk out of the tunnel and onto the barge.</li>
<li>Get it to shore, and as far from the water as you can.</li>
<li>Proceed with archeology, discover it turns off when less than 20 liters of water are present.</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</div>

